1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,975
Over the past forty episodes, we've taken this system apart,

2
00:00:03,975 --> 00:00:06,143
one real problem at a time.

3
00:00:06,143 --> 00:00:11,201
Today, I'd like to bring those pieces back together—and show you how

4
00:00:11,201 --> 00:00:13,008
they work as one journey.

5
00:00:13,008 --> 00:00:16,572
Let me start with a question I hear all the time:

6
00:00:16,572 --> 00:00:19,488
is there still a future in airline ticket sales?

7
00:00:19,488 --> 00:00:20,598
I believe there is.

8
00:00:20,598 --> 00:00:23,928
But it won't look like the business we used to know.

9
00:00:23,928 --> 00:00:28,357
Simply selling whatever seats happen to be available is getting harder.

10
00:00:28,357 --> 00:00:33,188
The real opportunity now is planning: knowing which route is worth taking

11
00:00:33,188 --> 00:00:38,019
on, when to secure seats, and how to sell them without losing

12
00:00:38,019 --> 00:00:40,032
control of inventory or profit.

13
00:00:40,032 --> 00:00:42,199
That's why I built this system.

14
00:00:42,199 --> 00:00:45,913
In this episode, I'd like to walk through it with you,

15
00:00:45,913 --> 00:00:49,008
step by step, and explain why every part is there.

16
00:00:49,008 --> 00:00:54,887
We will follow one complete business journey—from the first data source and

17
00:00:54,887 --> 00:00:59,862
route decision, through procurement, daily planning, inventory, channels, pricing, orders, ticketing,

18
00:00:59,862 --> 00:01:02,576
after-sales service, cash, and profit.

19
00:01:02,576 --> 00:01:07,550
Along the way, I will show what each function is for,

20
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:12,073
where its data comes from, how the system uses it,

21
00:01:12,073 --> 00:01:17,500
and how every result can be traced back when something goes wrong.

22
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,926
Now that the industry problem and the system goal are clear,

23
00:01:21,926 --> 00:01:26,353
we can follow the order in which the business actually happens.

24
00:01:26,353 --> 00:01:31,181
The first step is not to rush into a model or build

25
00:01:31,181 --> 00:01:31,986
a dashboard.

26
00:01:31,986 --> 00:01:36,815
It is to bring together the facts scattered across stores, ticketing, inventory,

27
00:01:36,815 --> 00:01:38,827
after-sales service, and finance.

28
00:01:38,827 --> 00:01:42,851
If the underlying data does not use the same definitions,

29
00:01:42,851 --> 00:01:47,679
every later judgment about routes, inventory, and profit can be wrong from

30
00:01:47,679 --> 00:01:48,484
the start.

31
00:01:48,484 --> 00:01:52,320
The data may come from O.T.A.

32
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:54,717
store orders, G.D.S.

33
00:01:54,717 --> 00:02:00,471
or supplier ticketing results, airline schedules and disruption notices, internal sales details,

34
00:02:00,471 --> 00:02:05,265
inventory ledgers, customer-service tickets, and payment or settlement records.

35
00:02:05,265 --> 00:02:10,060
Business users may also upload Excel or C.S.V.

36
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:10,539
files.

37
00:02:10,539 --> 00:02:16,293
The system first standardizes the route, flight, departure date, amount, order number,

38
00:02:16,293 --> 00:02:17,731
and inventory batch.

39
00:02:17,731 --> 00:02:22,526
It then checks for missing fields, format errors, duplicate records,

40
00:02:22,526 --> 00:02:23,965
and failed links.

41
00:02:23,965 --> 00:02:29,718
Data that fails validation remains at the intake gate and cannot carry

42
00:02:29,718 --> 00:02:31,636
an error further downstream.

43
00:02:31,636 --> 00:02:36,499
Only after intake is complete does the cockpit have a trustworthy base

44
00:02:36,499 --> 00:02:36,905
table.

45
00:02:36,905 --> 00:02:41,768
It brings opportunities, risks, pending work, and exceptions into one operating view,

46
00:02:41,768 --> 00:02:46,226
while allowing every indicator to trace back to its source record.

47
00:02:46,226 --> 00:02:50,684
At this point, we do not yet have a procurement decision.

48
00:02:50,684 --> 00:02:55,548
We have a factual base from which a decision can be made.

49
00:02:55,548 --> 00:03:00,411
The next step is to use those facts to answer the first

50
00:03:00,411 --> 00:03:03,248
operating question: which routes are worth pursuing?

51
00:03:03,248 --> 00:03:08,324
With a unified data base, the system can make its first decision.

52
00:03:08,324 --> 00:03:13,399
Planned pre-purchase cannot begin by asking how many seats to buy.

53
00:03:13,399 --> 00:03:17,206
It must first confirm which routes have sustained demand,

54
00:03:17,206 --> 00:03:21,858
which departure dates concentrate that demand, what prices the market accepts,

55
00:03:21,858 --> 00:03:26,934
whether supply is stable, and how much cost the team can carry.

56
00:03:26,934 --> 00:03:30,740
Deciding whether a route is worth operating comes first,

57
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:35,816
because experience alone can lead either to unsold inventory or missed opportunities.

58
00:03:35,816 --> 00:03:41,794
This stage reuses the orders, issued tickets, cancellations, and refunds standardized in

59
00:03:41,794 --> 00:03:43,289
the previous chapter.

60
00:03:43,289 --> 00:03:48,768
It also adds price and supply information from G.D.S.

61
00:03:48,768 --> 00:03:51,259
sources, airlines, O.T.A.

62
00:03:51,259 --> 00:03:57,237
platforms, and suppliers, together with demand calendars for holidays, exhibitions, school terms,

63
00:03:57,237 --> 00:03:58,732
and other events.

64
00:03:58,732 --> 00:04:05,208
The system aligns them by route, departure date, and advance-sales day to

65
00:04:05,208 --> 00:04:10,688
produce demand curves, price bands, supply-stability indicators, and risk warnings.

66
00:04:10,688 --> 00:04:15,848
This stage must deliver four explicit outcomes: whether to operate the route,

67
00:04:15,848 --> 00:04:19,717
when to secure seats, how many seats to secure,

68
00:04:19,717 --> 00:04:22,297
and the maximum acceptable procurement cost.

69
00:04:22,297 --> 00:04:27,457
The procurement direction becomes valid only after a person confirms all four.

70
00:04:27,457 --> 00:04:31,327
Once the route and total resource volume are fixed,

71
00:04:31,327 --> 00:04:36,486
the question changes from whether to operate to how this block of

72
00:04:36,486 --> 00:04:40,356
seats should be sold on every day before departure.

73
00:04:40,356 --> 00:04:44,414
Once the route, procurement quantity, and cost are confirmed,

74
00:04:44,414 --> 00:04:48,472
the system must move beyond a total-volume decision.

75
00:04:48,472 --> 00:04:53,883
Thirty days, fifteen days, and three days before departure require very different

76
00:04:53,883 --> 00:04:57,040
pricing, sales-speed, and inventory-protection strategies.

77
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,549
The sales model turns the procurement conclusion into daily targets,

78
00:05:01,549 --> 00:05:06,058
so the team knows how many seats to sell today,

79
00:05:06,058 --> 00:05:10,116
at what price, and how much inventory to protect.

80
00:05:10,116 --> 00:05:14,916
The model takes the procurement quantity and cost, flight date,

81
00:05:14,916 --> 00:05:20,196
historical sales curve, current transaction price, market quotes, channel order progress,

82
00:05:20,196 --> 00:05:23,556
cancellations and refunds, and the target profit.

83
00:05:23,556 --> 00:05:29,316
For every departure date, the system creates a daily sales calendar with

84
00:05:29,316 --> 00:05:34,116
a volume target, reference price, protected inventory, and priority channels.

85
00:05:34,116 --> 00:05:38,916
Each target is assigned to a date and an owner.

86
00:05:38,916 --> 00:05:44,574
Actual daily sales, transaction prices, and remaining inventory return to the same

87
00:05:44,574 --> 00:05:45,046
plan.

88
00:05:45,046 --> 00:05:50,704
The system compares plan with reality and determines whether the gap comes

89
00:05:50,704 --> 00:05:54,948
from changing demand, an excessive price, insufficient inventory allocation,

90
00:05:54,948 --> 00:05:56,834
or weak channel execution.

91
00:05:56,834 --> 00:06:01,549
The sales calendar is therefore not created once and forgotten;

92
00:06:01,549 --> 00:06:04,378
it is recalibrated with real outcomes.

93
00:06:04,378 --> 00:06:10,037
When a gap appears, the team still needs to know what action

94
00:06:10,037 --> 00:06:14,752
to take, which leads to deeper analysis and scenario comparison.

95
00:06:14,752 --> 00:06:19,276
The daily plan shows where performance has moved away from target,

96
00:06:19,276 --> 00:06:24,211
but a gap does not automatically mean the price should be cut.

97
00:06:24,211 --> 00:06:29,146
Slow sales may come from the price, a channel that has not

98
00:06:29,146 --> 00:06:34,492
opened enough volume, incorrectly allocated inventory, or even a flight-time change that

99
00:06:34,492 --> 00:06:35,725
has weakened demand.

100
00:06:35,725 --> 00:06:39,427
Deep analysis takes the deviation from the previous stage,

101
00:06:39,427 --> 00:06:43,128
brings the related factors together, explains the cause first,

102
00:06:43,128 --> 00:06:45,184
and then proposes an action.

103
00:06:45,184 --> 00:06:49,514
The system takes the demand window from route strategy,

104
00:06:49,514 --> 00:06:55,287
cost and remaining seats from procurement and inventory, sales progress from orders,

105
00:06:55,287 --> 00:06:59,136
and market prices from fare and comparison modules.

106
00:06:59,136 --> 00:07:04,909
It then applies minimum-profit, price-limit, sales-prohibition, and approval rules.

107
00:07:04,909 --> 00:07:10,682
Desensitized and structured data may enter intelligent analysis, but every recommendation must

108
00:07:10,682 --> 00:07:16,456
include its reasoning: why seats should be added, a price protected or

109
00:07:16,456 --> 00:07:20,786
reduced, a channel adjusted, or the sales pace changed,

110
00:07:20,786 --> 00:07:25,597
and how the action may affect volume, revenue, gross profit,

111
00:07:25,597 --> 00:07:27,040
and inventory clearance.

112
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:29,851
Recommendations enter a review area first.

113
00:07:29,851 --> 00:07:34,067
Historical backtesting or scenario comparison shows the possible outcome,

114
00:07:34,067 --> 00:07:39,688
and only after a business user confirms the choice does it become

115
00:07:39,688 --> 00:07:41,093
an executable task.

116
00:07:41,093 --> 00:07:46,714
The system has now converted a detected gap into a proposed action.

117
00:07:46,714 --> 00:07:52,336
The next question is whether real seat inventory exists to execute that

118
00:07:52,336 --> 00:07:57,020
action, so the business chain naturally moves into inventory preparation.

119
00:07:57,020 --> 00:08:02,473
The strategy may define how to sell, but it can only be

120
00:08:02,473 --> 00:08:05,654
executed when the inventory base is true.

121
00:08:05,654 --> 00:08:11,107
After procurement is confirmed, seats move from a contract or quotation into

122
00:08:11,107 --> 00:08:12,470
managed sales resources.

123
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:17,924
The system must know the route, flight, date, and booking class for

124
00:08:17,924 --> 00:08:23,377
every inventory batch, together with how many seats are occupied, sold, allocated,

125
00:08:23,377 --> 00:08:24,740
and still available.

126
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:31,305
Initial inventory may come from an airline or supplier seat-block confirmation,

127
00:08:31,305 --> 00:08:37,323
a procurement contract, a P.N.R., or an allocation list.

128
00:08:37,323 --> 00:08:42,247
A procurement user may also enter it after confirmation.

129
00:08:42,247 --> 00:08:46,623
Orders and issued tickets create holds and deductions.

130
00:08:46,623 --> 00:08:52,094
Cancellations, refunds, exchanges, and procurement adjustments release or transfer seats.

131
00:08:52,094 --> 00:08:58,659
Group business also contributes group requests, confirmed passenger counts, and internal allocations.

132
00:08:58,659 --> 00:09:04,677
The system creates seat-block inventory by flight and procurement batch,

133
00:09:04,677 --> 00:09:10,148
while group inventory separately records applications, confirmations, allocations, and balances.

134
00:09:10,148 --> 00:09:12,972
Every inventory change creates a ledger entry.

135
00:09:12,972 --> 00:09:17,813
The system uses those entries to calculate the true sellable quantity and

136
00:09:17,813 --> 00:09:21,847
raises warnings when too many seats remain close to departure,

137
00:09:21,847 --> 00:09:26,285
when seats are allocated twice, or when allocation approaches the limit.

138
00:09:26,285 --> 00:09:31,126
The seat resource is now ready, but the system still cannot accept

139
00:09:31,126 --> 00:09:35,564
an order until it knows where the inventory will be sold,

140
00:09:35,564 --> 00:09:39,598
who owns the order, and who will issue the ticket.

141
00:09:39,598 --> 00:09:43,632
The next stage is therefore stores, channels, and ticketing routes.

142
00:09:43,632 --> 00:09:46,131
Inventory answers what can be sold.

143
00:09:46,131 --> 00:09:49,878
Stores and channels answer where it will be sold,

144
00:09:49,878 --> 00:09:53,626
who receives the order, and who issues the ticket.

145
00:09:53,626 --> 00:09:58,207
The same ticket may be sold through several O.T.A.

146
00:09:58,207 --> 00:10:03,204
stores, direct channels, or corporate customers, and it may be issued by

147
00:10:03,204 --> 00:10:04,037
different suppliers.

148
00:10:04,037 --> 00:10:09,034
If an order has only a number but no source, settlement relationship,

149
00:10:09,034 --> 00:10:14,448
or owner, ticketing, after-sales service, and reconciliation will all become mixed together.

150
00:10:14,448 --> 00:10:20,873
Store and account data comes from platform onboarding records and internal account

151
00:10:20,873 --> 00:10:21,409
lists.

152
00:10:21,409 --> 00:10:27,834
Channel, supplier, settlement-policy, and service-hour data comes from cooperation agreements.

153
00:10:27,834 --> 00:10:33,188
Contacts come from customer, supplier, platform, and internal role directories.

154
00:10:33,188 --> 00:10:39,078
Ticketing routes combine the airline, route, booking class, price policy, cost,

155
00:10:39,078 --> 00:10:40,684
and handling priority.

156
00:10:40,684 --> 00:10:47,110
The system turns these records into stable mappings so an incoming order

157
00:10:47,110 --> 00:10:52,464
can immediately identify its source, responsibility relationship, and processing path.

158
00:10:52,464 --> 00:10:56,554
Orders that meet automatic-ticketing conditions enter the corresponding queue.

159
00:10:56,554 --> 00:11:01,054
Orders requiring judgment enter a work list with complete supporting data.

160
00:11:01,054 --> 00:11:05,962
When an exception occurs, the service ticket can reach the responsible person

161
00:11:05,962 --> 00:11:06,371
directly.

162
00:11:06,371 --> 00:11:11,280
We now know who handles the order, but we must still determine

163
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:16,188
which policy applies, what the true cost is, and what selling price

164
00:11:16,188 --> 00:11:17,416
should be used.

165
00:11:17,416 --> 00:11:22,324
Otherwise, the route may be correct while the quote is still wrong.

166
00:11:22,324 --> 00:11:27,737
An order with a source and a handling path still cannot be

167
00:11:27,737 --> 00:11:32,698
completed immediately, because an airline price is not an isolated number.

168
00:11:32,698 --> 00:11:38,111
Taxes, incentives, booking class, applicable dates, sales restrictions, and exchange or refund

169
00:11:38,111 --> 00:11:40,817
rules all change the true cost.

170
00:11:40,817 --> 00:11:45,328
The policy and fare modules use the earlier route, inventory,

171
00:11:45,328 --> 00:11:50,741
and channel information to determine whether a quote is valid and whether

172
00:11:50,741 --> 00:11:52,996
the sale can preserve profit.

173
00:11:52,996 --> 00:11:59,036
Policies may come from airline and supplier documents, email, back-office notices,

174
00:11:59,036 --> 00:12:00,546
or formal interfaces.

175
00:12:00,546 --> 00:12:04,573
Fares and taxes come from G.D.S.

176
00:12:04,573 --> 00:12:08,097
sources, airlines, suppliers, and O.T.A.

177
00:12:08,097 --> 00:12:08,600
searches.

178
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:12,123
Market prices come from authorized comparison tasks.

179
00:12:12,123 --> 00:12:18,164
Internal procurement cost, target profit, minimum selling price, and sellable inventory define

180
00:12:18,164 --> 00:12:19,674
the operating boundary.

181
00:12:19,674 --> 00:12:25,714
The system converts written policies into computable conditions and converts quotes from

182
00:12:25,714 --> 00:12:30,244
different sources into complete costs that can be compared.

183
00:12:30,244 --> 00:12:36,224
During quoting, ordering, and ticketing, the system checks whether the policy applies.

184
00:12:36,224 --> 00:12:42,205
When market prices change, it combines inventory progress with the profit floor

185
00:12:42,205 --> 00:12:48,185
to generate a repricing recommendation, while preserving the supporting evidence and approval

186
00:12:48,185 --> 00:12:48,684
result.

187
00:12:48,684 --> 00:12:52,671
The customer, processor, and price are now determined.

188
00:12:52,671 --> 00:12:58,651
The strategy must next enter a real order and complete ticketing and

189
00:12:58,651 --> 00:12:59,648
ticket verification.

190
00:12:59,648 --> 00:13:05,858
Route decisions, sales plans, inventory, channels, and prices must eventually become business

191
00:13:05,858 --> 00:13:06,375
execution.

192
00:13:06,375 --> 00:13:12,585
A recommendation without an owner, deadline, and returned result remains only a

193
00:13:12,585 --> 00:13:13,102
recommendation.

194
00:13:13,102 --> 00:13:19,312
An order that cannot show whether it is waiting for payment, ticketing,

195
00:13:19,312 --> 00:13:22,934
or verification cannot support a customer commitment.

196
00:13:22,934 --> 00:13:29,144
Strategy tasks and the order center turn earlier decisions into traceable actions.

197
00:13:29,144 --> 00:13:34,119
Strategy tasks come from route, model, repricing, and inventory modules.

198
00:13:34,119 --> 00:13:37,104
Orders come from O.T.A.

199
00:13:37,104 --> 00:13:41,084
stores, direct channels, corporate customers, or manual entry.

200
00:13:41,084 --> 00:13:45,063
Passenger and itinerary information comes from booking data.

201
00:13:45,063 --> 00:13:49,043
Payment status comes from payment or settlement systems.

202
00:13:49,043 --> 00:13:56,008
P.N.R.s, ticket numbers, and issuance results come from G.D.S.

203
00:13:56,008 --> 00:13:58,993
sources, airlines, suppliers, or ticketing staff.

204
00:13:58,993 --> 00:14:04,963
The system assigns a unique identifier to every task and order and

205
00:14:04,963 --> 00:14:09,440
records its evidence, owner, deadline, and every status change.

206
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:13,897
The order enters the queue selected by the ticketing route.

207
00:14:13,897 --> 00:14:16,571
After issuance, the P.N.R.

208
00:14:16,571 --> 00:14:21,919
and ticket number are written back, and the verification module checks the

209
00:14:21,919 --> 00:14:25,484
order, passenger, flight, and ticket against one another.

210
00:14:25,484 --> 00:14:30,833
Missing data, channel failure, processing timeout, or a ticket mismatch immediately moves

211
00:14:30,833 --> 00:14:33,061
the case into exception handling.

212
00:14:33,061 --> 00:14:38,409
This completes the path from plan to ticketing, but the chain is

213
00:14:38,409 --> 00:14:43,757
not over because a delivered ticket may still face a schedule change,

214
00:14:43,757 --> 00:14:45,540
exchange, cancellation, or refund.

215
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:51,045
An issued ticket completes the first delivery, but it does not establish

216
00:14:51,045 --> 00:14:52,880
the final business result.

217
00:14:52,880 --> 00:14:57,009
A schedule change may require notification, rebooking, or refund.

218
00:14:57,009 --> 00:15:02,514
A cancellation changes the ticket, inventory, revenue, and cost at the same

219
00:15:02,514 --> 00:15:02,973
time.

220
00:15:02,973 --> 00:15:08,936
The after-sales module carries the original order and ticket number forward so

221
00:15:08,936 --> 00:15:13,524
every later change remains attached to the same business transaction.

222
00:15:13,524 --> 00:15:17,582
Disruption information comes from airlines, G.D.S.

223
00:15:17,582 --> 00:15:20,626
sources, supplier notices, or manual confirmation.

224
00:15:20,626 --> 00:15:26,206
Exchange and refund requests come from customers, stores, and service teams.

225
00:15:26,206 --> 00:15:31,786
Applicable rules come from the original fare policy and supplier conditions.

226
00:15:31,786 --> 00:15:37,873
Refunds, fees, receivables, and cash received come from payment platforms, settlement statements,

227
00:15:37,873 --> 00:15:40,409
bank transactions, and finance updates.

228
00:15:40,409 --> 00:15:45,989
The original order provides the passenger, flight, ticket number, selling price,

229
00:15:45,989 --> 00:15:51,569
and procurement cost, allowing the system to decide whom to notify,

230
00:15:51,569 --> 00:15:57,657
which solution to execute, how much to refund, and who owns the

231
00:15:57,657 --> 00:15:58,164
work.

232
00:15:58,164 --> 00:16:04,882
The system links every disruption and after-sales ticket back to the original

233
00:16:04,882 --> 00:16:10,050
order and continuously records notifications, proposed solutions, owners, refund progress,

234
00:16:10,050 --> 00:16:11,600
and final outcomes.

235
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:17,801
The finance module then aggregates sales, procurement cost, taxes, fees, refunds, receivables,

236
00:16:17,801 --> 00:16:19,352
and cash received.

237
00:16:19,352 --> 00:16:25,553
It distinguishes estimated, confirmed, and paid states and calculates the real profit

238
00:16:25,553 --> 00:16:28,137
for the order and route.

239
00:16:28,137 --> 00:16:34,855
The after-sales state has returned to the order, but one question remains:

240
00:16:34,855 --> 00:16:41,056
after a cancellation or refund, was the corresponding inventory released as well?

241
00:16:41,056 --> 00:16:44,650
The previous chapter changed order and ticket states.

242
00:16:44,650 --> 00:16:50,040
This chapter ensures that the change does not stop on the order

243
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:50,489
page.

244
00:16:50,489 --> 00:16:55,430
If an order occupies a seat but inventory is not reduced,

245
00:16:55,430 --> 00:16:57,227
the system may oversell.

246
00:16:57,227 --> 00:17:02,617
If a cancelled order does not release inventory, a sellable seat becomes

247
00:17:02,617 --> 00:17:03,067
unavailable.

248
00:17:03,067 --> 00:17:08,457
Order and inventory synchronization must therefore answer exactly which order changed which

249
00:17:08,457 --> 00:17:11,152
inventory batch, and at what time.

250
00:17:11,152 --> 00:17:17,239
The link uses the order number, inventory batch, flight date, booking class,

251
00:17:17,239 --> 00:17:22,312
passenger count, order status, ticketing status, and cancellation, refund, exchange,

252
00:17:22,312 --> 00:17:24,341
and external callback events.

253
00:17:24,341 --> 00:17:30,429
These inputs come from the order center, ticketing result, after-sales ticket,

254
00:17:30,429 --> 00:17:32,965
channel interface, and inventory ledger.

255
00:17:32,965 --> 00:17:39,053
The system performs a hold, deduction, release, or transfer according to the

256
00:17:39,053 --> 00:17:45,140
business state, and uses an event identifier to recognize duplicate callbacks so

257
00:17:45,140 --> 00:17:48,184
the same action cannot run twice.

258
00:17:48,184 --> 00:17:51,542
Every change produces an inventory ledger entry.

259
00:17:51,542 --> 00:17:57,778
Front-end sellable quantity is aggregated from that ledger, and an operator can

260
00:17:57,778 --> 00:18:01,136
trace the specific order, time, and action.

261
00:18:01,136 --> 00:18:06,413
A single order now forms a closed loop across ordering, ticketing,

262
00:18:06,413 --> 00:18:09,291
after-sales service, inventory, and finance.

263
00:18:09,291 --> 00:18:15,047
The next step moves beyond one transaction and aggregates all business for

264
00:18:15,047 --> 00:18:20,324
the day to determine whether overall operations have deviated from plan.

265
00:18:20,324 --> 00:18:25,123
After a single order closes its loop, management must return to the

266
00:18:25,123 --> 00:18:26,322
full operating picture.

267
00:18:26,322 --> 00:18:31,121
At the end of a day, the number of tickets sold is

268
00:18:31,121 --> 00:18:31,921
not enough.

269
00:18:31,921 --> 00:18:36,320
The team must know whether sales were timely, inventory is healthy,

270
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:41,119
refunds are increasing, cash has arrived, and profit has met the target.

271
00:18:41,119 --> 00:18:45,917
Operating statistics take the results of the earlier modules and answer two

272
00:18:45,917 --> 00:18:50,716
questions: where did we deviate today, and what should we change first

273
00:18:50,716 --> 00:18:51,116
tomorrow?

274
00:18:51,116 --> 00:18:56,538
The system aggregates route strategy, sales plans, channel orders, ticketing time,

275
00:18:56,538 --> 00:19:00,482
inventory ledgers, after-sales refunds, cash, and profit.

276
00:19:00,482 --> 00:19:03,933
Planned targets come from the sales model.

277
00:19:03,933 --> 00:19:08,369
Actual outcomes come from order, ticketing, and finance records.

278
00:19:08,369 --> 00:19:13,792
Exception causes come from tasks, work tickets, and the operating process.

279
00:19:13,792 --> 00:19:19,707
Because the same flight, inventory batch, order, and ticket identifiers were used

280
00:19:19,707 --> 00:19:25,622
throughout, an aggregate can still drill down to its source record instead

281
00:19:25,622 --> 00:19:28,580
of ending at a single number.

282
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:34,784
The nightly review compares plan and reality by route, store, channel, date,

283
00:19:34,784 --> 00:19:35,818
and owner.

284
00:19:35,818 --> 00:19:40,471
It identifies demand, price, channel, inventory, and execution problems,

285
00:19:40,471 --> 00:19:46,158
then generates tomorrow's tasks for adding supply, repricing, clearing inventory,

286
00:19:46,158 --> 00:19:49,260
chasing ticket issuance, and collecting receivables.

287
00:19:49,260 --> 00:19:54,947
Operating results now feed back into the next day's plan.

288
00:19:54,947 --> 00:19:59,600
But as more users view, change, and export data,

289
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:05,804
the system must also control who may perform each action and whether

290
00:20:05,804 --> 00:20:07,872
every action remains traceable.

291
00:20:07,872 --> 00:20:13,581
Once the business chain is running, risk comes not only from business

292
00:20:13,581 --> 00:20:16,435
decisions but also from system use.

293
00:20:16,435 --> 00:20:19,766
Who can view, edit, approve, or export?

294
00:20:19,766 --> 00:20:23,571
Can data be recovered after an operational mistake?

295
00:20:23,571 --> 00:20:26,902
These questions directly affect every earlier feature.

296
00:20:26,902 --> 00:20:31,659
Permissions, export, cleanup, and logs are therefore not secondary pages;

297
00:20:31,659 --> 00:20:37,368
they form the governance layer that keeps the system operating over time.

298
00:20:37,368 --> 00:20:43,574
Employee, role, organization, and account-validity data determine the access scope.

299
00:20:43,574 --> 00:20:50,908
Strategy, inventory, order, ticket, after-sales, and finance data determine the objects that

300
00:20:50,908 --> 00:20:53,164
can be acted upon.

301
00:20:53,164 --> 00:20:59,934
Login, query, modification, approval, execution, export, and cleanup actions create audit records.

302
00:20:59,934 --> 00:21:06,140
The system restricts view, edit, approval, and execution permissions by role.

303
00:21:06,140 --> 00:21:11,218
An export keeps its filter conditions and generation record.

304
00:21:11,218 --> 00:21:17,988
A cleanup displays the affected scope and requires the corresponding authorization before

305
00:21:17,988 --> 00:21:19,680
it can continue.

306
00:21:19,680 --> 00:21:25,486
Critical operations record the account, time, object, state before and after the

307
00:21:25,486 --> 00:21:27,421
change, and final result.

308
00:21:27,421 --> 00:21:33,226
This supports collaboration and handover while also allowing the team to identify

309
00:21:33,226 --> 00:21:37,097
responsibility and recover data when an exception occurs.

310
00:21:37,097 --> 00:21:42,902
Only after internal data, process, and permission boundaries are clear is the

311
00:21:42,902 --> 00:21:48,224
system ready to exchange real outcomes with airlines, G.D.S.

312
00:21:48,224 --> 00:21:50,643
providers, suppliers, O.T.A.

313
00:21:50,643 --> 00:21:52,578
platforms, and payment services.

314
00:21:52,578 --> 00:21:56,932
That is the role of the external interface layer.

315
00:21:56,932 --> 00:22:01,473
The earlier modules control how the system decides and acts internally,

316
00:22:01,473 --> 00:22:06,014
but the final result in aviation often occurs outside the system.

317
00:22:06,014 --> 00:22:10,967
A page that says ticket issued does not prove that the airline

318
00:22:10,967 --> 00:22:13,444
or supplier actually issued a ticket.

319
00:22:13,444 --> 00:22:18,398
A page that says refunded does not prove that cash has arrived.

320
00:22:18,398 --> 00:22:23,351
The external interface layer sends internal orders and tasks outward and brings

321
00:22:23,351 --> 00:22:25,828
formal results back into the system.

322
00:22:25,828 --> 00:22:33,645
Integration materials come from the other party's official interface documentation, field dictionary,

323
00:22:33,645 --> 00:22:39,657
account authorization, key or certificate, test environment, and callback specification.

324
00:22:39,657 --> 00:22:46,873
Business data comes from search, ordering, ticketing, refund, payment, and notification interfaces.

325
00:22:46,873 --> 00:22:53,487
Runtime evidence includes request identifiers, response codes, callback content, failure reasons,

326
00:22:53,487 --> 00:22:55,892
and external-platform states.

327
00:22:55,892 --> 00:23:03,107
An adapter converts different external fields into one internal object and handles

328
00:23:03,107 --> 00:23:10,323
authentication, requests, callbacks, retries, monitoring, and transfer to a person when automation

329
00:23:10,323 --> 00:23:10,924
fails.

330
00:23:10,924 --> 00:23:16,563
External order numbers, ticket numbers, prices, and payment results are written back

331
00:23:16,563 --> 00:23:22,672
to the original business record and continue to drive inventory, after-sales service,

332
00:23:22,672 --> 00:23:23,612
and finance.

333
00:23:23,612 --> 00:23:29,251
Without a formal response, the state may only remain processing or pending

334
00:23:29,251 --> 00:23:29,721
verification.

335
00:23:29,721 --> 00:23:33,950
Every feature now has its own input and output,

336
00:23:33,950 --> 00:23:39,589
but the outputs still have to be proven as one continuous transaction.

337
00:23:39,589 --> 00:23:44,288
The next stage uses one case to connect every feature.

338
00:23:44,288 --> 00:23:48,835
If every page uses a different route, inventory batch, and order,

339
00:23:48,835 --> 00:23:53,796
each feature may appear to produce a result while the full system

340
00:23:53,796 --> 00:23:55,449
proves nothing about continuity.

341
00:23:55,449 --> 00:24:01,236
The purpose of an end-to-end case is to place the outputs of

342
00:24:01,236 --> 00:24:04,543
the first fourteen chapters into one business chain.

343
00:24:04,543 --> 00:24:09,504
The same route, flight, inventory batch, and order must keep the same

344
00:24:09,504 --> 00:24:11,984
business identifiers from beginning to end.

345
00:24:11,984 --> 00:24:17,876
The case starts with historical sales, market prices, and procurement quotes to

346
00:24:17,876 --> 00:24:21,313
create a route decision and procurement plan.

347
00:24:21,313 --> 00:24:27,205
After procurement is confirmed, the system establishes inventory and a daily sales

348
00:24:27,205 --> 00:24:31,133
plan, then links stores, channels, policies, and fares.

349
00:24:31,133 --> 00:24:36,534
An incoming order creates a seat hold, ticket issuance, and verification.

350
00:24:36,534 --> 00:24:42,427
A schedule change or refund is then introduced so the team can

351
00:24:42,427 --> 00:24:47,337
observe the resulting changes in inventory, refunds, cash, and profit.

352
00:24:47,337 --> 00:24:52,738
The source material may come from historical spreadsheets, airlines and suppliers,

353
00:24:52,738 --> 00:24:58,630
sales platforms, ticketing systems, customer service, and finance, but once it enters

354
00:24:58,630 --> 00:25:04,031
the system it must be linked by flight, inventory batch, order,

355
00:25:04,031 --> 00:25:05,504
and ticket identifiers.

356
00:25:05,504 --> 00:25:11,255
Finally, operating statistics aggregate the plan, execution, exceptions, and outcome for the

357
00:25:11,255 --> 00:25:12,213
same case.

358
00:25:12,213 --> 00:25:16,527
Audit logs prove who completed each action and when.

359
00:25:16,527 --> 00:25:20,840
We can now see whether the features truly connect.

360
00:25:20,840 --> 00:25:26,111
Yet running the chain once is still not enough for delivery.

361
00:25:26,111 --> 00:25:30,425
Final acceptance must test the normal path, exception paths,

362
00:25:30,425 --> 00:25:33,300
and state continuity after a restart.

363
00:25:33,300 --> 00:25:38,552
Once one case has connected all features, final acceptance no longer checks

364
00:25:38,552 --> 00:25:40,302
buttons page by page.

365
00:25:40,302 --> 00:25:45,554
It checks whether the whole business can move from input to result.

366
00:25:45,554 --> 00:25:50,805
The acceptance standard has three parts: the normal business path completes end

367
00:25:50,805 --> 00:25:56,057
to end, invalid data or external failures are stopped at the correct

368
00:25:56,057 --> 00:26:01,308
point, and data, tasks, and states remain consistent after the system restarts.

369
00:26:01,308 --> 00:26:07,900
The normal path uses a desensitized but complete business sample covering route,

370
00:26:07,900 --> 00:26:12,843
procurement, inventory, order, ticket, after-sales service, and finance.

371
00:26:12,843 --> 00:26:18,885
Exception paths use a copy or test environment with missing fields,

372
00:26:18,885 --> 00:26:24,928
insufficient inventory, out-of-range prices, channel failures, invalid ticket numbers,

373
00:26:24,928 --> 00:26:27,674
and payments without a response.

374
00:26:27,674 --> 00:26:34,266
Before each step, the expected input, action, state, and result are written

375
00:26:34,266 --> 00:26:34,815
down.

376
00:26:34,815 --> 00:26:40,308
The team then verifies quantities, amounts, owners, and external responses.

377
00:26:40,308 --> 00:26:46,288
The system meets the delivery condition only when the normal chain completes,

378
00:26:46,288 --> 00:26:52,267
the exception chain is stopped correctly, and every state remains traceable after

379
00:26:52,267 --> 00:26:52,765
restart.

380
00:26:52,765 --> 00:26:58,745
The functional order is now clear: standardize data, decide the route and

381
00:26:58,745 --> 00:27:04,724
procurement plan, create sales plans and recommendations, prepare inventory and channel policies,

382
00:27:04,724 --> 00:27:10,206
handle orders, ticketing, and after-sales service, synchronize inventory and finance,

383
00:27:10,206 --> 00:27:15,189
and finally close the loop through review, governance, external responses,

384
00:27:15,189 --> 00:27:17,680
and end-to-end acceptance.

385
00:27:17,680 --> 00:27:23,216
Looking back at this chain, the system does not place a dozen

386
00:27:23,216 --> 00:27:25,061
features side by side.

387
00:27:25,061 --> 00:27:30,597
It makes the result of one step become the input of the

388
00:27:30,597 --> 00:27:31,059
next.

389
00:27:31,059 --> 00:27:32,904
Route decisions drive procurement.

390
00:27:32,904 --> 00:27:35,672
Procurement creates inventory and sales plans.

391
00:27:35,672 --> 00:27:38,901
Channels and policies make that inventory sellable.

392
00:27:38,901 --> 00:27:44,899
Orders and tickets record real execution, and after-sales activity continues to change

393
00:27:44,899 --> 00:27:46,744
inventory, cash, and profit.

394
00:27:46,744 --> 00:27:52,701
Those results return to the operating review and create the next day's

395
00:27:52,701 --> 00:27:57,284
tasks for adding supply, repricing, clearing inventory, chasing ticket issuance,

396
00:27:57,284 --> 00:27:58,658
and collecting receivables.

397
00:27:58,658 --> 00:28:02,324
Permissions and logs keep the whole process traceable.

398
00:28:02,324 --> 00:28:07,365
External interfaces bring formal results from airlines, suppliers, O.T.A.

399
00:28:07,365 --> 00:28:11,031
platforms, and payment services back into the system.

400
00:28:11,031 --> 00:28:16,530
Every feature now has a defined position, and we can explain why

401
00:28:16,530 --> 00:28:21,112
it must sit between the functions before and after it.

402
00:28:21,112 --> 00:28:26,616
A valuable aviation intelligence system is not defined by the number of

403
00:28:26,616 --> 00:28:27,992
pages or figures.

404
00:28:27,992 --> 00:28:31,203
Every data item must have a source.

405
00:28:31,203 --> 00:28:33,955
Every state must have an owner.

406
00:28:33,955 --> 00:28:37,166
Every recommendation must become an actionable task.

407
00:28:37,166 --> 00:28:41,753
Every result must trace back to the original business record.

408
00:28:41,753 --> 00:28:47,257
From data intake to final acceptance, that is a complete operating loop

409
00:28:47,257 --> 00:28:50,468
that people can understand, execute, and verify.

410
00:28:50,468 --> 00:28:54,818
Thank you for following this series and for staying with me through

411
00:28:54,818 --> 00:28:56,269
these forty-one episodes.

412
00:28:56,269 --> 00:28:59,894
You are always welcome to reach out, share your thoughts,

413
00:28:59,894 --> 00:29:01,344
or leave a comment.